drm: kernel order for YCbCr ranges; BT.709 for HD video without colour information

1. wlr_drm_color_range listed full before limited, while the kernel's drm_color_range has limited
   as 0: every plane asked for as limited range went out as full range (washed-out video in direct
   scanout), and the other way about.
2. A client that sends no colour representation (Chromium) got BT.601 for everything. Video of HD
   size is taken to be BT.709, as players do, in the DRM backend and in the GLES2 renderer alike, so
   scanout and composition still agree.

diff -ru a/backend/drm/atomic.c b/backend/drm/atomic.c
--- a/backend/drm/atomic.c	2026-10-21 17:34:51.664526241 -0500
+++ b/backend/drm/atomic.c	2026-10-21 17:34:51.723240802 -0500
@@ -487,7 +487,11 @@
 	// between direct scanout and composition. Default to BT601/limited to
 	// match GLES2 renderer behavior, or use values from output state if
 	// WLR_OUTPUT_STATE_COLOR_ENCODING_AND_RANGE is committed.
-	enum wlr_color_encoding encoding = WLR_COLOR_ENCODING_BT601;
+	// Without colour information, video of HD size is taken to be BT.709, as
+	// players do (and as the GLES2 renderer does, see render/egl.c).
+	enum wlr_color_encoding encoding =
+		(output_state->committed & WLR_OUTPUT_STATE_BUFFER) && output_state->buffer != NULL &&
+		output_state->buffer->height >= 720 ? WLR_COLOR_ENCODING_BT709 : WLR_COLOR_ENCODING_BT601;
 	enum wlr_color_range range = WLR_COLOR_RANGE_LIMITED;
 	if (output_state->committed & WLR_OUTPUT_STATE_COLOR_REPRESENTATION) {
 		encoding = wlr_color_representation_v1_color_encoding_to_wlr(
